**Digital Leads ****&**** Staff Digital Development**

**Training Plan — 2025–27**

Graeme Wright  ·  Digital Pedagogy Coach  ·  Quality & Innovation Team  ·  Weston College Group

Prepared: April 2026  ·  Review: July 2026 (Governance Review 1)

# **1. Purpose ****&**** Scope**

This plan sets out how Weston College will build and sustain staff digital capability across all 34 curriculum areas. It operates across two interconnected tracks:

- Digital Leads — named staff (one per curriculum area) who act as the embedded digital development contact for their team, run Teams Health Checks, and support peer upskilling. This is the primary focus of the plan.

- All teaching and support staff — a broader expectation of digital skills progression, tracked via the Jisc Discovery Tool for baseline and Microsoft Learn, LinkedIn Learning, or Jisc pathways for development.

Learner digital skills are addressed in a separate framework (Section 5), using Century as the primary tracking platform for General Digital Skills, alongside area-specific Specialist Digital Skills mapping agreed with each HoA.

# **2. Design Principles**

- Low burden: all CPD resources are free-to-access and self-paced. No additional cost to the college.

- Evidence-based: every pathway produces a verifiable output — a completion certificate, a module badge, or a log entry signed off by a line manager.

- Rooted in practice: development is tied directly to what Digital Leads need to do in their area, not abstract digital skills in isolation.

- Accountable through line management: Digital Leads report progress to their HoA. All-staff digital upskilling is tracked via line managers using the Jisc baseline as the starting point. Individual Jisc data is not shared centrally — progress is reported in aggregate and through line manager sign-off.

- Progressive: pathways align to the Digital Learning Pyramid (Digital Foundations → Digital Inclusion → Digital Innovation) so progression is clear and cumulative.

- Equity-aware: access to CPD must not be contingent on individual staff having premium accounts. All recommended platforms have free or institution-licensed tiers.

# **3. Digital Leads Programme**

## **3.1  What is a Digital Lead?**

A Digital Lead is a teaching or support staff member, nominated within each curriculum area, who takes on a defined set of digital responsibilities alongside their main role. This is not a separate management grade — it is a recognised professional contribution.

Digital Leads are distinct from Digital Champions, who are a separate learner-facing initiative to be developed in a future phase. The Digital Lead role is entirely staff-facing.

## **3.2  Role Responsibilities**

- Run the Teams Digital Health Check for their area termly (once trained).

- Act as first point of contact for digital queries within their team — triage before escalating to the DPC.

- Share good practice within their area: signpost colleagues to resources, lead short Teach Meet slots.

- Support the HoA to complete digital data in Business Plans and Quality Improvement Plans.

- Complete their own Digital Lead CPD pathway (see Section 3.4) and maintain a brief evidence log.

- Attend Digital Leads network meetings (one per term, facilitated by the DPC).

