# **4. All-Staff Digital Skills Development**

## **4.1  Rationale**

Digital upskilling for all teaching staff is increasingly central to the college’s quality agenda. As accountability for learner digital skills grows — moving towards parity with English and Maths expectations in funded programmes — staff confidence must keep pace. The college cannot hold learners to a standard that staff have not themselves reached.

The approach below is designed to be light-touch on administration, credible as an evidence base for governance, and manageable within existing line management structures.

## **4.2  Baseline: Jisc Discovery Tool**

The Jisc Discovery Tool provides a validated, sector-standard baseline for staff digital capability aligned to the Digital Teaching Professional Framework (DTPF). It is the recommended starting point for all teaching staff.

- The DPC will communicate the Jisc Discovery Tool to TLAMs with guidance on facilitating completion within their teams.

- Completion is voluntary at individual level, but TLAMs are asked to encourage team engagement and record the number of completions in their area.

- Individual results remain private to the member of staff. Line managers are not sent individual scores.

- Aggregate data at area level (percentage who have completed, broad distribution across DTPF stages) is shared with the DPC termly and reported in governance documentation.

- The DPC will use aggregate data to target coaching and CPD offers — not to identify or performance-manage individuals.

## **4.3  Development Pathways — All Teaching Staff**

Following the Jisc baseline, staff self-direct their development using the platforms below, supported by coaching from the DPC and their Digital Lead. Line managers are asked to include digital development as a standing item in annual appraisals and in-year reviews for staff identified as needing support.

| **DTPF Stage** | **Recommended Platform** | **Suggested Pathway** | **Indicative Time** | **Evidence Output** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Stage 1 (Exploring)** | Microsoft Learn | "Microsoft 365 Fundamentals" + "Create accessible content with M365" | 3–5 hours | Module completion certificates |
| **Stage 1 (Exploring)** | Jisc | "Developing your digital literacies" — Modules 1–3 | 4–6 hours | Module completion log |
| **Stage 2A (Adopting)** | Microsoft Learn | "Collaborate with Teams" + "Excel / Forms for educators" | 4–6 hours | Completion badge + one classroom application logged |
| **Stage 2A (Adopting)** | LinkedIn Learning | "Teaching with Technology" learning path (where licensed) | 5–8 hours | Certificate of completion |
| **Stage 2B (Leading)** | Jisc | "Learning and Teaching" strand — full pathway | 8–12 hours | Pathway certificate + one peer CPD contribution |
| **Stage 2B (Leading)** | Microsoft Learn | "AI for Educators" + "Power Automate for productivity" | 5–7 hours | Completion badge + documented classroom impact |
| **Stage 3 (Innovating)** | Jisc / LinkedIn | Self-directed — agreed with DPC and line manager | Flexible | Project output or external presentation |

## **4.4  Tracking Model — Line Manager Accountability**

Individual Jisc data is not centrally accessible — this is by design and consistent with GDPR principles. The tracking model therefore operates through line managers:

- TLAMs record the number of staff in their area who have completed the Jisc baseline (not scores — just completion Y/N).

- Staff who begin a development pathway log completion of modules in the template below, co-signed by their line manager at review points.

- The DPC collates area-level data termly and reports to quality leadership in aggregate.

- Areas with consistently low engagement are flagged to the relevant TLAM for a targeted conversation — not to HR.

