# **5. Learner Digital Skills — Century ****&**** Specialist Mapping**

## **5.1  Two-Track Model**

Learner digital skills development operates on two distinct tracks, both of which must be planned, tracked, and evidenced:

- General Digital Skills — tracked via Century. Century provides an Initial Assessment (IA) that places learners at Entry 3 or Level 1, followed by diagnostic assessments and targeted nugget-based learning. This is the primary mechanism for evidencing and improving general digital skills across all programmes.

- Specialist Digital Skills — industry-specific tools and practices defined collaboratively with HoAs. These are tracked through a bespoke mapping agreed between the DPC and each HoA, and evidenced through programme work, portfolios, and learning walk observations.

## **5.2  Century: General Digital Skills**

Century is the agreed platform for tracking and improving General Digital Skills for learners. The model works as follows:

| **Initial Assessment** | All learners complete the Century IA at enrolment or in the first weeks of their programme. The IA places them at Entry 3 or Level 1. Results are visible to the teaching team and the DPC via the Century dashboard. |
| --- | --- |
| **Diagnostic assessments** | Teachers or the DPC set up diagnostic assessments through the year, organised by theme (e.g. digital communication, data handling, online safety). These identify specific gaps within a learner’s level. |
| **Nugget learning** | Learners are assigned or self-select Century ‘nuggets’ — short, adaptive learning units — to address the gaps identified in diagnostics. Progress is automatically tracked in the Century dashboard. |
| **Progress review** | At each formal review point (minimum termly), teachers and the DPC review Century dashboard data to identify learners making insufficient progress. Interventions are planned in collaboration with the HoA. |
| **Absent learners** | Where learners are absent, Century content remains accessible asynchronously via their digital learning environment (Teams). Teachers share resources in advance, and absent learners are expected to access content and nuggets independently. Progress is captured and evidenced through the Century dashboard. |
| **Training: Train the Trainer** | The DPC will arrange a dedicated training session between Century (Sam) and Libby Heath-Tavener as the English & Maths lead. Following this, Libby will deliver in-team training to E&M colleagues. The DPC supports this process and extends the model to other areas. |

**Note on JISC vs Century:**

The Jisc Discovery Tool is designed for teaching staff and measures digital capability against the DTPF framework. Century is a learner-facing platform measuring General Digital Skills (aligned to EDS / DigComp). They serve different purposes and different audiences — both should be in use, but should not be conflated or used interchangeably.

## **5.3  Specialist Digital Skills — Area-by-Area Mapping**

In addition to the Century general track, every curriculum area must identify and track the specialist digital skills that are intrinsic to their vocational or academic field. These are agreed between the DPC and each HoA through the mapping conversation, and reviewed annually.

